{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't ask the AI 'are you sure?'. Check its output against the source, and test the checkers on your own data so you pick one from a scoreboard, and know what it can't catch. Nobody said what it costs, so I can't take it to the board yet.","claims":["When an AI gives you work, don't ask it to confirm itself. Compare what it produced against the original source material. Before relying on any automated checker, run each candidate on your own data, rank them by how well they performed, and pick the best one. Also write down which kinds of mistakes it still misses.","Don't trust an AI's own confirmation of its work. Verify what it produced against the original source material, and before relying on any checking tool, try it on your own real data, compare the candidates by their results, and learn which kinds of errors each one misses.","When you want to know whether an AI's output is right, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Compare what it produced against the original source material. Before relying on any automated checker, run several candidates on your own real data, rank them by how many errors each catches, choose the best, and note which kinds of errors it still misses.","Don't just ask the AI to confirm its own work. Compare what it produced against the original source material. Before relying on any automated checker, try several of them on your own real data, rank them by how well they perform, and note which kinds of mistakes each one misses. Find out what the checking costs before you propose it to leadership."],"picks":["none","evaluations","security","security","evaluations","none","output-quality","security"],"why":["The claim is about choosing a checker by testing candidates on your own data and noting what it misses. That isn't grounding (C), a fixed-judge eval of changes (A), or running a safety check (E). Only the 'compare to the source' line touches C.","The claim is about building a trustworthy eval: score output against the source, choose the judge by testing candidates on your own data, and record what it still misses. That is the eval-with-a-fixed-judge idea, not grounding or safety.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own confirmation, and to try each checking tool on your own real data to see what it misses. That is C: output alone can't show safety, so you run the check.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own confirmation, to run an independent check against the source, and to test checkers on real data before relying on them. That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'.","The claim is about choosing and validating the automated checker (judge) on your own data and measuring it, which is eval practice. It is not about safety (H) or grounding (F).","Only the first sentence is about grounding. The rest is about choosing a checker: bake off several on your own data, rank them, note what each misses, and cost it. That is not C's 'stay connected to the truth, make the gap visible', and it is not A's 'fixed judge to measure change'.","The core move is to check output against the original source rather than the agent's self-confirmation, which is grounding; the checker-ranking part is a method for doing it, not the eval idea of a fixed judge measuring change.","The claim says not to trust an AI's output on its self-confirmation. It says to run an actual check against the source and to test any checker on real data. That matches E: you can't tell by looking at the output, so you have to run a check."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"The AI states claims it has no source for in the same confident tone, and asking it to confirm itself proves nothing, so the check has to go back to the source, and you pick that check by testing candidates and reading catch rate against false alarms. Ask any vendor what their check misses. It took fifty-five minutes of setup and never gave a cost figure.","claims":["When the AI tells you something, don't ask the AI whether it's right, because it will sound just as sure either way. Check the claim against the original source instead. To choose which checking method to use, try several on real examples and compare how many errors each one catches against how often it flags things that are actually fine.","When the AI gives you a factual claim, don't ask the AI whether it's right, because it will sound just as sure either way. Check the claim against the original source instead. To choose which checking method to use, try several on real examples and compare how many errors each catches against how often it wrongly flags correct material.","When the AI gives you facts, don't ask it whether it's sure; compare them against the original source material. Choose how to do that comparison by trying several candidate checks on cases where you know the answer, and prefer the one that catches the most errors without raising too many false warnings.","When the AI tells you something, don't ask the AI whether it's right, because it will sound just as sure either way. Check the claim against the original source instead. To choose which check to use, try several on real examples and compare how many errors each catches against how often it wrongly flags correct work. Before buying a checking tool, ask the vendor what kinds of errors it misses."],"picks":["security","security","security","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The AI's output can't show whether it's right, since it sounds equally sure either way. The colleague would act by running an external check and testing which check works. The claim doesn't carry G's idea that the agent fills gaps it can't ground.","The claim says you can't judge AI output from how confident it sounds, so you need an actual check, and it picks the check by testing it on real examples. That matches idea H (you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check). Idea F is about keeping the agent connected to ground truth, which is a different point.","The AI's confidence doesn't show whether it's right, so you need an external check, and you pick that check by testing it on real examples. That matches 'you can't tell by looking at output; run the check'.","The claim is about confident-but-wrong output and checking it against the original source (grounding). G is about safety, and the method-comparison part is only supporting detail.","The claim centers on not trusting the AI's self-assessment and running an independent check, with checks tested on catch and false-flag rates and vendors probed for misses, which matches 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check'. It is not C, because the claim is about verifying output rather than keeping the agent connected to the ground, and not D, because the claim is not about measuring whether a change improved the work.","Don't trust the AI's self-assessment, run an external check against source and measure what it catches and misses — the 'can't tell safety/correctness from output, run the check' idea, not grounding (F) since the focus is verification practice.","Checking facts against original source material (not asking the AI if it's sure) is grounding; the eval-style 'try candidate checks on known cases' is secondary, but the core action is staying connected to the source truth.","Checking facts against original source material is grounding: stay connected to the truth rather than asking the agent if it's sure; the trying-checks-on-known-cases part is secondary."],"score":0.375,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust the AI asking itself 'are you sure?': check against the source, and test your checks on your own data before you rely on one. Ten unchecked steps at 85% each gets you about one right in five. Nobody said what any of it costs.","claims":["Never accept an AI's own 'are you sure?' as verification. Compare its output to the original source material, and before relying on any check, run it on data where you already know the right answer to confirm it catches errors. Also remember that chaining many steps that are each mostly reliable (85%) leaves the final result wrong most of the time, so add a real check at each step.","When an AI tells you its work is right, don't take its word, including when you ask it to double-check itself. Compare the result against the original source material, and before you trust any checking step, try it on your own examples where you already know the right answer. Also, in a long chain of steps where each is only mostly reliable, errors multiply, so a long unchecked chain will usually end up wrong.","When an AI checks its own work by being asked if it's sure, that proves nothing. Compare its output against the original source material instead. Before trusting any checking step, run it on data where you already know the right answers and see whether it catches the errors. Also, chaining many steps that are each only mostly reliable makes the final result mostly wrong, so add checks between steps rather than only at the end.","When an AI's output feeds the next step, don't ask the AI to re-confirm its own answer. Compare each result against the original source material, and first try your checking method on data where you already know the right answers, so you know it catches errors. Errors compound across a chain: ten steps that are each 85% reliable leave only about a 20% chance the whole chain is right."],"picks":["security","security","security","output-quality","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Verify with an external check (against source, tested on known answers) rather than the AI's self-confirmation; running the check is the practice, not M5's grounding or relying on the agent's own word.","The claim is about verification: don't have the AI self-confirm, check against source, and test the checker on known answers. That matches 'you can't tell if the agent is safe from its output; you need a check, and the practice is running it'. It is not D (evals measuring improvement) and not F (grounding).","The claim is about not trusting the agent's self-check and running real checks validated on known answers at each step, which matches 'you can't tell safety from output; run the check'.","Verifying against original source material, testing checks on known-answer data, and per-step checks all keep the agent connected to ground truth, which is grounding. A is about running a check, but the claim centers on source comparison.","The claim says you can't trust an AI's own word that its output is right, so you need an independent check, and that check has to be validated on known answers before you rely on it. That matches D's 'can't tell by looking at the output; run the check'. G is about grounding gaps and B is about measuring change, so neither fits as well.","The claim is about not trusting output or self-checks, and about running independent checks, including testing the checker on known answers. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'.","The claim is about verification: output alone or self-assessment can't show it's right, so you need a real check, one you've tested against known errors and placed between steps.","The claim says output or self-assurance can't verify work, so you need a real check that you run and have tested. E states that idea; A is about measuring change over time, and H is only about grounding."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cfo-sceptic-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Small errors compound across steps, so an unchecked workflow is mostly wrong, and asking the AI 'are you sure?' is not a check: you need a separate check against the source, picked by measuring it on your own data, and it should state what it cannot catch. I'd still want a cost and payback figure before taking it to the board.","claims":["Don't trust an automated multi-step process just because you asked the AI to double-check itself. Add a separate verification step that compares the output with the original source material, test candidate checks on your own real data to see which catches errors best, and write down which kinds of errors that check will miss.","When you chain several AI steps, assume mistakes pile up and the final result is probably wrong. Don't ask the AI to confirm its own work. Add a separate verification step that compares the output with the original source material, choose that step by testing candidates on your own real data, and write down which kinds of errors it won't catch.","When you chain several agent steps, assume mistakes pile up and the end result is probably wrong. Don't trust it because the AI says it's confident when you ask. Add a separate step that compares the output against the original source material, choose that step by testing candidates on your own real data, and write down what it can't catch.","When you chain several AI steps together, assume mistakes pile up and the end result is probably wrong. Don't ask the AI to double-check itself. Add a separate verification step that compares output to the original source material, choose that step by testing candidates on your own real data, and write down which kinds of mistakes it will miss."],"picks":["security","security","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Errors compound, so don't trust self-checks; add an independent check against source, tested on real data, with known gaps documented. That is 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","The claim says you can't trust output or self-confirmation, so you add and run a separate check and note what it misses. That matches the 'you need a way to check' idea, not grounding the agent in sources.","The claim says an AI's own double-check isn't enough and you need a separate check you actually run, with its blind spots written down. That is idea C: you can't judge by looking at the output, and the practice is running the check.","The claim is about staying connected to the source material by verifying output against the original source, and about knowing what the check will miss. That is grounding. F is about checking safety, not source fidelity.","The core move is checking output against the original source material, which is grounding. The rest (don't self-check, test candidate checkers, note what they miss) supports making the ungrounded gap visible.","The claim says to check the output against the original source material in a separate step, because the chain will otherwise fill gaps with confident errors. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth.","The claim's core move is a verification step that compares output to the original source material, and it distrusts the agent's stated confidence. That is staying connected to the ground truth, which is grounding. It is not B, which is about safety checks, or E, which is about eval criteria.","Checking output against original source material (not the agent's self-reported confidence) is grounding: staying connected to the truth so errors in the gap become visible."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't pick one hallucination check and trust it. Race several on your own output, because each misses things another catches, and keep the judge as a file with its known limits written in. Anything you run unchecked across an org needs a check between proposal and apply.","claims":["Don't rely on a single test to catch made-up facts in an AI's output. Run several different tests against the same output, since each one misses cases the others catch. Save the grading criteria as a file that also lists what it can't detect. For any automated job running across the whole organization, put a verification step between when the AI suggests a change and when that change is applied.","When you set up a check for made-up facts in an AI's work, don't rely on one method. Run several different checks on the same output, since each catches errors the others miss. Store the judging criteria in a file that also lists what that judge is known to get wrong. And if an AI process runs unattended across the organization, put a verification step between the moment it suggests a change and the moment the change is applied.","Don't rely on a single test to catch made-up facts in AI output. Run several different tests on the same output, since each one catches things the others miss. Store the grading criteria in a file along with a written list of what it can't catch. For any AI process running unsupervised across the organization, add a verification step between the AI suggesting a change and that change being applied.","Don't rely on a single method to catch fabricated claims in AI output. Run several different checks side by side, since each one catches errors the others miss. Store the grader as a file that also lists what it is known to get wrong. For any AI work that runs unattended across the organization, add a verification step between the AI suggesting a change and that change being applied."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is to run multiple checks, document what each can't catch, and gate AI-proposed changes with verification, because output alone can't show whether it's safe. That is D. It never mentions staying connected to ground truth (H) or a fixed judge measuring improvement (F).","The claim is to run several different checks and put a verification step before changes are applied, instead of trusting the output. It never says to connect the agent to a source of truth, so it isn't G.","The claim is about running independent checks and putting a verification step before changes apply. It says nothing about staying connected to ground truth (G) or keeping a fixed judge to measure improvement (D).","The claim is about running checks, including a verification step before changes are applied, because output alone doesn't show whether it can be trusted. It says nothing about staying connected to the truth (E), and the fixed judge file is only a side detail, so F doesn't fit.","The claim is about running checks, several of them, plus a verification gate before changes apply, rather than trusting the output. It says nothing about staying connected to ground truth, so G doesn't fit.","The claim's actions are to run several checks, document what they can't detect, and verify before applying changes. That is 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It says nothing about connecting the agent to a source of truth (D), so it isn't grounding.","The claim is about building layered verification, including a gate before unattended changes apply, because you can't judge an agent's output by looking at it. That is B's idea (run the check), not H's (a fixed judge that measures whether a change improved the work) or G's (grounding).","The claim is about running independent checks and putting a verification gate before changes apply, which is D's 'run the check' practice. It never says to connect the agent to ground truth (C), and it isn't about measuring improvement with a fixed judge (A)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust an agent's output by gut: build a test with known planted errors, run several candidate checkers against it, and keep the one that scores best. Agents invent things with no source, and the invented text sounds just as confident as the rest.","claims":["Before relying on what an agent tells you, make a test document with mistakes you put in on purpose, try several different reviewing setups on it, and use whichever one catches the most of them. Expect the agent to make up details that have no source, and don't take its confident tone as a sign they're right.","Before relying on what an agent produces, make a test set where you have deliberately inserted known mistakes, try several different reviewers on it, and adopt whichever one catches the most. Expect the agent to make up statements with nothing behind them, and expect those to read as assured as the accurate parts.","Before you rely on what an AI agent tells you, make a practice set of documents in which you have deliberately hidden known mistakes. Try several different review methods on that set, and use the one that catches the most. Remember that an agent will state made-up details that have no source in the same assured tone as true ones, so how sure it sounds tells you nothing.","Before relying on an AI agent's work, build a test set where you've deliberately inserted known mistakes, try out several different reviewers on it, and adopt whichever one catches the most. Also remember that when the agent has no real source to draw on, it makes things up and the fabricated parts read as confidently as the accurate ones, so you can't judge by tone."],"picks":["output-quality","security","security","evaluations","output-quality","evaluations","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim's core is that confident tone is no signal of truth, because the agent fills gaps with made-up details. That matches the grounding idea. The practice-set and method-comparison part is only a supporting tactic.","Confident tone tells you nothing about whether the output is right, so you have to run a check. You test the check on seeded errors and keep the one that catches the most.","The claim's core is that an agent's output and confident tone can't tell you if it's right, so you build a check (planted errors, comparing reviewers) before relying on it.","The core action is building a seeded-error test set and comparing reviewing setups to keep the best, which is measuring against defined criteria to see which change works better. The fabrication warning is only a side note, and it lacks the grounding idea of staying connected to a source.","The claim's core is that the agent fills gaps with confident, plausible fabrications and you must make those visible before relying on it. That is the grounding idea. The seeded-error reviewer test is just one way to do that.","Seeding known errors and choosing the reviewer that catches the most is how you build and validate a fixed, repeatable judge, which is the eval idea. The line about confident fabrication is only background.","The claim's closing point is that without a real source the agent fills the gap with confident, plausible fabrication, which is the grounding idea. The seeded-mistakes test set is only the method for checking it, and it doesn't match E's fixed judge or H's safety check.","The claim's distinctive point is that without a real source the agent fabricates, and tone can't tell you which parts are wrong. That is the grounding idea. The seeded-error test and reviewer comparison is only a mechanism and doesn't match E or G's core claim."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Chaining agent steps without a check compounds errors, so end-to-end quality ends up much worse than per-step quality. Instead of guessing which checker works, run a few on your own output, score them, and keep the winner as a judge file that says what it can't catch.","claims":["When one automated step feeds the next, small mistakes in each step pile up, so the final result is far worse than any single step looks. Next week, before trusting a multi-step pipeline, try several different ways of checking the work on output I already have, grade how well each one catches problems, keep the best one as my standing reviewer, and write down in a file what that reviewer is known to miss.","When one agent step feeds the next with nothing verifying it in between, each step's mistakes pile up, so the whole chain ends up far less reliable than any single step looks. Next week: before trusting a checker, try several candidate checkers on output I already have, score how well each one catches real problems, keep the best one, and write down in a file what it fails to catch.","When one automated step feeds the next, each step's mistakes pile up, so the final result is far worse than any single step looks. Before relying on a chain, try several candidate reviewers on your own real output, score how well each one catches problems, keep the best, and save it as a file that also lists the kinds of problems it misses.","When one automated step feeds the next, mistakes pile up, so the final result is far worse than any single step looks. Before relying on a checker, try several candidate checkers on your own real output, grade how well each does, and keep the best one as a saved reviewer file that also lists the kinds of problems it will miss."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","security","security","evaluations","evaluations","none","evaluations"],"why":["The action is to build and calibrate a fixed, repeatable judge (try checkers, grade them, keep the best as the standing reviewer, document its blind spots). That is the eval idea. The compounding-error framing is only the motivation.","The colleague would act by trying several checks, scoring each on output they already have, and keeping the best as a fixed reviewer with its known misses written down. That is building a repeatable eval with a stable judge.","The claim says unverified chain output can't be trusted and that you need to pick, run and understand a check. That is B's 'you need a way to check'. It doesn't mention ground truth (G) or a fixed judge that measures change (F).","The claim is about not trusting unverified agent output, then building and calibrating a check and recording its blind spots. That is F's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. G is about measuring whether a change improved the work, which the claim doesn't address.","Trying candidate judges on real output, scoring them, and keeping the best as a fixed file is the eval idea: a repeatable, fixed judge that measures whether output is better.","Compounding errors across chained steps, then testing candidate judges on real output, scoring them, keeping the best fixed judge, and documenting its blind spots, is the eval idea: measuring output against criteria with a fixed, validated judge.","The claim is about picking and validating a checker, with errors compounding across chained steps. It doesn't carry grounding (A), fixed-judge measurement of whether a change improved the work (H), or running a check because output can't show safety (F).","The claim is about building and calibrating a fixed judge (a saved reviewer file) on real output, graded against criteria, so changes can be measured. That is the eval idea, not grounding or safety."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-cto-builder-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Without a check, agents fill gaps with fluent, plausible guesses, and errors compound across steps (ten steps at 85% is about one in five correct). So you test checkers on your own output with planted errors, keep the best, and put a check between propose and apply.","claims":["When an agent works in several steps, don't trust it to be right just because it sounds right: small error rates multiply across steps, so most long runs end up wrong. Before relying on a checker, test it by planting known mistakes in your own work and seeing whether it catches them. Use the checker that catches the most, and run it on each proposed change before that change is applied.","Before you let an agent act on its own proposal, run a separate verification step in between. Pick that verifier by testing candidates on your own work with errors you deliberately inserted, and use whichever catches the most. Don't trust unchecked output, because a long chain of mostly-right steps ends up wrong most of the time.","Don't trust an agent's output just because it reads well: where it lacks real information it invents believable answers, and mistakes pile up over a long chain of steps. Before letting it act on anything, put a verification step in between. Pick that verification by seeding your own real output with known mistakes and seeing which verifier catches them best.","Before letting an AI agent act on its own work, put a separate check in the path. Test that checker first by planting known mistakes in your own real output and seeing whether it catches them. Use the one that catches the most, and run it at every step, because small error rates multiply across steps."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","output-quality","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim says not to trust output that sounds right and to verify with a checker you've tested, running it before each change is applied. That is idea C: you can't judge safety from the output, so you run the check.","The claim is about not trusting output that sounds right, planting known mistakes to test the checker, and running the check on each change. That is 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check', not an eval with a fixed judge (H) or grounding (G).","The claim says to insert a separate check, because unchecked output can't be trusted and chained errors compound. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It isn't B, which is about measuring whether a change improved the work with a fixed judge.","Don't trust unchecked output; run a separate verification check before the agent acts, rather than judging safety from output alone.","The core is that where the agent lacks real information it fills the gap with plausible, wrong answers, which is the grounding idea. The verifier-picking detail is a way of applying it.","The claim says you can't judge an agent's output by how well it reads, so you need to run a verification check before it acts. Choosing the verifier by seeding known mistakes is part of running that check.","The claim is about putting a separate check in the agent's path, validating it with planted errors, and running it at every step. That is the 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check' idea. It says nothing about a fixed judge for measuring whether a change improved the work.","Planting known mistakes to test a separate checker and running it at every step is the 'run the check' practice for verifying agent output, not eval-based comparison (G)."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"AI can sound right without being right, so you can't just ask it 'are you sure?': the check has to go back to the source, and someone has to own that checking step before anything gets applied. I'd add that the module should say more about who that person is.","claims":["When the AI tells you something, don't accept its word or ask it to confirm itself. Check the claim against the original material it should come from, and name one specific person who is responsible for doing that check before anyone acts on the result.","When the AI tells you something, don't trust it because it sounds convincing or because it says it's sure. Send the claim back to the original material it came from and compare. Before anyone acts on the AI's output, name one specific person who is responsible for doing that comparison.","When the AI gives you an answer that sounds convincing, don't accept its own word that it's correct. Verify against the original material, and name one specific person who is responsible for that verification before anyone acts on the answer.","When an AI gives you an answer, don't accept its own assurance that it's correct. Compare the answer against the original source material, and name one specific person who is responsible for doing that comparison before the answer is used."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Checking AI claims against the original source to catch confident-but-wrong gap-filling is grounding (stay connected to the truth).","Checking AI claims against the original source (staying connected to the ground) instead of trusting confident output is the grounding idea.","Checking claims against original source material is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than trusting the agent's confident fill-in. The 'name a responsible person' part is an add-on, not a different idea.","Checking AI claims against the original source material is grounding: staying connected to the truth so the gap where it fills in confidently isn't missed.","The claim is about a convincing answer that may not be tied to the original material, so check it against the source. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, because the model fills gaps confidently and plausibly but wrongly.","Don't trust the agent's confident answer; check it against the original source, since without access to the ground it fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","The claim is to check the answer against the original source material instead of trusting the AI's assurance. That is staying connected to the ground truth, which is grounding.","The claim is about checking an answer against the original source instead of trusting the AI's confidence, which is grounding. It is about correctness, not safety (F)."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust an agent's 'are you sure?': a real check goes back to the sources, and a long chain of unchecked steps ends up mostly wrong, so a human check before the action is part of the design. I'm still fuzzy on the jargon like judge, precision and recall.","claims":["When an agent says it double-checked its work, don't take that as verification. Make the check go back to the original sources, and keep a person reviewing before any step that acts on the result, because errors build up across a long chain of unchecked steps.","When an agent says it double-checked its work, don't take that as verification. Make the check go back to the original sources, and keep a person reviewing before any step that acts on the world, because many unchecked steps in a row make the final result mostly wrong.","When an agent says it double-checked its work, don't count that as verification. Make it go back to the original sources and confirm each fact, and put a person's review in front of any step that takes real action, because errors build up across a long run of unchecked steps.","When an AI agent says it double-checked its work, don't take that as verification. Make it go back to the original sources and confirm each point, and put a person's review in before any step that acts on the results, because mistakes pile up across a long run of unchecked steps."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The agent's own claim of having double-checked is not evidence, so you run an independent check (source confirmation, human review before acting). That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check', not the grounding idea that an agent fills gaps it can't reach.","The claim says not to trust the agent's self-reported check as verification, and to run real checks and add human review, which matches 'can't tell if the agent is safe from its output; run the check'.","The claim says to send the check back to the original sources instead of trusting the agent's own self-check. That is staying connected to the ground truth, which is the grounding idea.","Agent's self-claimed 'double-check' isn't real verification; the check must reconnect to original sources (ground truth) because unchecked gaps get filled confidently and wrongly — that is grounding.","Claim says 'I checked' isn't verification: independently check against sources and put human review before real actions — i.e., you need an actual check, not trust in output.","The claim says self-reported double-checking isn't verification and calls for independent checks (re-confirm against sources, human review before actions); D says you can't judge safety from output alone, you need to run a real check.","The claim is about staying connected to original sources and not trusting the agent's self-check, which is grounding. The human-review and error-accumulation points support it.","Claim says 'double-checked' isn't verification; check against original sources (grounding) so the agent doesn't fill gaps with plausible-but-wrong output."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the agent 'are you sure?' proves nothing; a real check goes back to the source files, and a good check names what it cannot catch. For anything that matters, the agent proposes, a person checks, and only then is it applied, so the practical question is who owns that check.","claims":["Don't accept the agent's own 'are you sure?' as verification. When the work matters, have the agent propose the change, have a named person re-check it against the original source files, and only then apply it. Decide up front who owns that check, and say what kinds of errors it can't catch.","Don't accept the agent's own reassurance when you ask it to confirm its work. Have it re-check against the original source material, and make the check state what kinds of mistakes it would miss. For anything important, the agent drafts a change, a named person reviews it, and only then is it put into effect, so decide ahead of time who that reviewer is.","Don't accept the agent's own 'are you sure?' as verification. Verify important work by comparing it against the original source files, and write down what that check can't detect. For anything consequential, have the agent only propose the change, have a named person review it, and apply it after that review. Decide up front who that reviewer is.","Don't accept the agent's own reassurance when you ask it to confirm its work. Have it re-check against the original source material, and make the check say what kinds of errors it would miss. For anything important, the agent drafts the change, a named human reviews it, and only after that is it applied. So before relying on an agent's output, decide who is responsible for that review."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Rejects the agent's self-check, requires an independent named checker against source, and names its blind spots — matching 'you can't tell it's safe from output; run the check'.","Rejects the agent's self-check and insists on running an independent, owned check (and naming what it can't catch) rather than trusting output or the agent's assurance.","Claim says don't trust the agent's self-confirmation, run an independent check that states its blind spots, and assign a human reviewer — matching 'can't tell safety from output; run the check.'","Don't trust the agent's self-confirmation; run an independent check that names its blind spots, with a named human reviewing before apply — that is running the check rather than trusting output (safety/verification), not grounding in source (E) or eval-judge fixed-measurement (H).","Don't trust the agent's self-reassurance; run an independent check (against source, with stated blind spots, plus human review) — safety can't be judged from output alone.","The claim is about not trusting the agent's self-reassurance and running an independent check, with a named human reviewer. That matches 'you can't tell if your agent is safe from its output, so run the check'.","Claim says the agent's self-check isn't verification, so run an external check against the source, name its blind spots, and have a named reviewer approve before applying; that is 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","The claim is about not trusting the agent's own output or self-check and running an independent check (against sources, with a named reviewer), which is idea A's practice of running a check."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"s1-hr-svp-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"An agent sounds just as sure when it has no source, so asking it \"are you sure?\" proves nothing; the check has to go back to the file, number or quote, and a person should own that check before anything is applied.","claims":["When an AI tells you something, don't trust it because it sounds confident or because it says yes when you ask if it's certain. Take the specific figure, name or quotation back to the original document and compare it yourself, and have a named person responsible for doing that comparison before you act on or apply anything the AI produced.","When an AI agent gives you a fact, figure or quotation, don't ask it to confirm itself, because it will sound equally certain whether or not it has real backing. Instead, open the original document, data or passage and compare it yourself. Make sure a named person is responsible for doing that comparison before any of the agent's output is used or acted on.","When an AI tells you something, don't trust it because it sounds confident or because it says yes when you ask 'are you certain?'. Verify by going back to the original file, figure or quotation it should have drawn from, and make sure a named person is responsible for doing that verification before any of its output is used.","When an AI tells you something, don't treat its confidence as evidence, and don't settle it by asking it to reassure you. Open the actual source it relied on and compare the specific figure, quote or file contents yourself. Make one named person responsible for doing that comparison before any of the AI's output is used or acted on."],"picks":["security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security"],"why":["The claim says don't trust confident output or the AI's self-assurance; verify specific figures, names and quotations against the source, with a named person doing the check. That is the 'run the check' practice from C. H is about keeping the agent connected to the ground truth.","Verifying the AI's specific figures, names and quotations against the original source is grounding: staying connected to the truth because a confident AI fills gaps plausibly and wrong.","The claim says to trace output back to the original source (file, figure, quotation) rather than trust confident-sounding output, which is grounding: staying connected to the truth when the agent can't reach it.","The claim says confident output isn't evidence and tells you to check it against the original source. That is grounding: staying connected to the underlying truth, because a gap gets filled confidently and wrongly.","The claim is about facts the agent states confidently without real backing, which a person catches by checking the original source. That is grounding: making the gap between what the agent says and what is true visible.","Don't trust the agent's own confidence; check against the original source to stay connected to ground truth, because it fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","The claim says an AI's output and confidence can't be trusted on their face, so someone must run an independent check against the source and own it. That matches F's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check', not A's point about agents staying connected to ground truth.","The claim says you can't judge an AI's output by how confident it sounds, so someone must actually run a check against the source. That is F's idea that the practice is running the check. A is about keeping the agent connected to the truth, which the claim doesn't address."],"score":0.625,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust one way of catching made-up claims: test several against your own material, score them, and keep a saved judge file that says what it catches and what it misses, so you know where a human still has to look.","claims":["When you need to stop an AI from inventing unsupported statements, don't settle on one checking method. Try several on your own real documents, score how well each one performs, and keep the best. Then write down in a saved reviewer file what that check reliably catches and what it misses, so next week you know which cases still need a person to read them.","When you need to catch an agent inventing facts, don't rely on a single checking method. Try several against your own documents, score how well each one does, and keep the best as a saved reviewer file that states what it reliably flags and what it misses. Use that file to decide which spots a person must still check by hand.","When you need to catch an AI inventing facts, don't rely on a single checking method. Try several on your own documents, give each a score, then keep the best one in a saved reviewer file that lists what it reliably flags and what it misses. Use that list to decide which parts a person must still read by hand.","When you need to catch an AI inventing facts, don't rely on a single checking method. Try several against your own documents, score how well each one does, and keep a written record of the best checker that lists what it reliably flags and what it misses, so you know which cases a person must still review by hand."],"picks":["security","security","evaluations","none","output-quality","security","none","none"],"why":["The claim is about building and validating a way to check for invented facts, because output alone can't show it. It then keeps a record of what the checker catches and misses, so people know what to review by hand.","The claim is about building and scoring checks on hallucinated output, and recording what each check misses so a person knows what to review by hand. That fits 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It doesn't fit B's fixed judge for measuring a change, or F's grounding the agent.","The claim is about building a repeatable judge. You score candidate checkers on real documents, keep the best, and save it as a fixed reviewer with its known coverage and gaps. That is the eval-with-a-fixed-judge idea, not just 'run a check'.","The claim is about comparing several checking methods, scoring them, and keeping the best with a note of its blind spots. It never says to stay connected to the source (B). It also doesn't say to run a check instead of waiting for certainty (C), or to hold a fixed judge so you can see whether a change helped (F).","Fabricated facts are the core of the claim, and the saved reviewer file shows what it catches and misses, so people check the gaps by hand. That is grounding, which makes the gap visible. It is not generic eval measurement (A).","The claim is about building and running a checking method, with its known blind spots, because output alone can't show whether the agent invented facts. It is not about grounding the agent in truth (E) or measuring whether a change improved the work (G).","The claim is about choosing the best of several fact-checking methods and keeping it as a reviewer file. It never says to stay connected to ground truth (A), to fix a judge and measure whether a change improved the work (F), or to check agent safety (H).","The claim teaches 'try several checkers, score them, keep the winner and its known misses'. That is not grounding (D), a fixed-judge eval (C), or safety checking (H), though it shares words with each."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Checking an agent's output against its sources is its own job, separate from writing it. Build a saved judge file, test a few candidate checks on your own material, and remember it only catches invented content, so a wrong source or a tilted framing is still on you.","claims":["When an agent writes something from your sources, have a separate, saved reviewer step compare the result to those sources, and try out several versions of that reviewer on your own documents before trusting one. Expect it to flag only made-up content; you still have to check that the sources themselves are right and that the framing isn't slanted.","Treat verifying an AI's work against the material it drew from as a separate task from producing the work. Save a reusable checker, try several candidate checks on your own documents, and keep in mind that it only flags made-up content: if the source itself is wrong or the framing is slanted, you still have to catch that yourself.","When an AI agent produces a document from source material, run a separate verification step afterward whose only job is to compare the document's statements to those sources. Keep that check as a reusable saved file, and try several versions of it on your own real material before settling on one. Don't treat it as proof of quality: it only flags statements the sources don't support, so you still have to judge whether the sources themselves are right and whether the overall slant is fair.","When an agent writes something from your source material, have a separate step check it against those sources, using a reusable saved check that you've tried out in several versions on your own material. Expect that check to catch only made-up content: you still have to verify yourself that the sources themselves are correct and that the framing isn't slanted."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","evaluations","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","evaluations"],"why":["A separate check comparing the document's statements to its sources, while noting that it can't judge whether the sources are true, is grounding. It keeps the output tied to the sources and makes unsupported statements visible.","The claim is about checking an agent's statements against source material so unsupported ones show up. The gap between output and sources is the grounding idea; it never describes an eval that measures whether a change improved the work.","A reusable, tried-out check run as a separate step against sources is an eval with a fixed judge; the caveat that it only catches fabrication (not bad sources or slanted framing) fits A's scope-limited measurement, not E's broader grounding discipline.","The claim is about checking an agent's writing against its sources so made-up content gets caught, while the user still verifies the sources and the framing. That is grounding, not a fixed-judge eval.","Verifying output against its source material and catching fabricated content is grounding; the caveat that it only catches made-up content, not wrong sources, matches B's gap-filling idea.","Checking AI output against its source material to catch made-up content is grounding: staying connected to the truth, with the check itself being a separate step.","The claim is about checking an agent's writing against its sources to catch invented content (grounding), and it says the check can't confirm the sources are right or the framing is fair.","The claim describes a saved, repeatable judge that checks output against defined criteria (the sources), and the colleague should test judge versions on their own documents. That is the eval idea, and the limits it names (source accuracy, framing) are what an eval doesn't cover."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't ask the agent 'are you sure?' — build a check that actually goes back to the sources, test a few on your own briefing, and keep the winner as a reusable judge file. It only catches invented content, so wrong sources and strategic calls are still on you.","claims":["When you want to confirm an agent's work is sound, don't just ask it to re-confirm itself. Build a separate review step that goes back to the original sources and flags anything that was made up. Try a few versions of that step on a real briefing, keep the best one, and save it as a file you can reuse. Know its limit: it catches fabricated content only, so you still have to judge whether the sources are right and whether the strategic decisions are sound.","When you want to verify an AI's work, don't just ask it to double-check itself. Build a separate check that sends it back to the original sources, try a few versions of that check on your own real task, and save the best one as a reusable file. This only catches made-up content, so you still have to judge whether the sources are right and whether the strategic decisions are sound.","When you want an AI to verify its own work, don't just ask it to double-check. Build a separate checking step that goes back to the original sources, try a few versions of it on a real example of your own, and keep the best one as a saved, reusable file. That check only catches made-up content; you still have to judge whether the sources are right and whether the strategic decisions are sound.","When you want an AI to verify its own work, don't just ask it to double-check. Set up a separate verification step that re-reads the original source material and compares the output against it. Try a few versions of that step on a real task of yours, pick the one that works best, and save it as a reusable instruction file. Remember it only catches made-up content; you still have to judge whether the sources are right and whether the strategic decisions are sound."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is a separate review step that goes back to the original sources and flags fabricated content, which is grounding (staying connected to truth and making the gap visible). It is not an eval, even though it mentions trying versions and keeping the best one.","The claim is a review step that goes back to the original sources and flags fabricated content, with the limit that it can't judge whether the sources are right. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth and making the gap visible.","Sending the AI back to original sources, plus the caveat that it only catches made-up content and not wrong sources, is the grounding idea: fabrication fills the gap where it can't reach the truth.","Sending the AI back to original sources to catch made-up content is grounding: staying connected to truth so gaps aren't filled with plausible fabrication.","The claim says to stop asking the AI to double-check its own output and build a separate, saved check that goes back to the sources. It also says that check has limits: it catches fabrication, and you still judge the rest. That is F's point about needing a way to check, not trusting the output.","The claim is about building a separate, reusable check instead of trusting the output or a casual 'double-check', then running it. It also says what the check can't cover.","The claim's core is a verification step that re-reads the original sources and compares the output to them, which catches fabricated gap-filling. That is grounding. The caveat about judging whether the sources are right fits A's 'truth out there' framing.","The claim's core is a check that re-reads the source and compares the output against it, which catches made-up content. That is grounding. It also says grounding can't tell you whether the sources or the strategy are right."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ingrid-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust a check because it sounds right: run several candidate checks on your own output, let the scoreboard pick the winner, and save it as a judge file that says what it misses. And grounded only means traceable to a source, not true, so the check has to touch the sources.","claims":["When you need a way to check your agent's work, don't pick the first test that seems reasonable. Write several different candidate tests, run them all on the output you already have, and keep whichever one performs best. Save that test as a file that also lists what it fails to catch. Remember that an answer tied to a cited source may still be wrong, so the test must go and look at the sources themselves.","When you need to check your agent's work, don't just pick a check that seems reasonable. Write several different candidate checks, run them all on your own outputs, and keep the one that scores best. Save it as a reusable file that also lists what it fails to catch. Also, when a result is backed by a source, that only shows you can trace it, not that it's correct, so have the check open the sources and compare them against the claim.","When you need a way to check your agent's output, don't pick one because it sounds reasonable. Write several different candidate checks, run them all on the same output, and keep the one whose results score best. Save that check as a file that also lists what it fails to catch. Also, an answer being tied to a cited source doesn't mean it's correct, so make the check open and compare against the actual sources.","When you need a quality check for the agent's work, don't just pick one that looks sensible. Write several different candidate checks, run them all on output you already have, and keep whichever one separates good from bad work best. Save it as a reusable file that also records what it fails to catch. Also, a claim that can be traced to a source isn't necessarily correct, so make the check actually go to the sources and verify them."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","none","none","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is about building a repeatable eval. You try candidate tests on existing output, keep the one that differentiates best, and save it as a fixed file with its known blind spots. That matches A (a fixed, repeatable check; differentiation as something it measures). Grounding (H) is only a side note about checking sources.","Candidate tests are scored against existing output and the best is kept as a fixed, repeatable check, which is the eval idea. Saving it with its known blind spots belongs to the same practice, while the sourcing warning is a detail within it.","Building several candidate checks, testing them on existing output to see which separates good from bad, and saving it as a reusable judge with known blind spots is eval design (a fixed, repeatable judge measuring output against criteria).","The claim's core is building a reusable, repeatable check by testing candidate judges on existing output and keeping the one that best separates good from bad. That is the eval idea. The source-verification line is a side detail, not the grounding idea that D describes.","The claim is about building an eval: pick a judge by testing candidates on the same output, then save it as a fixed, repeatable file with known blind spots. A is only the general point that you need some check, and H is only the grounding point.","The claim is mainly about choosing a check by testing candidates and saving it with its blind spots. It is not about grounding (the agent filling gaps when cut off from truth), fixed-judge evals for measuring change, or safety checks. The cited-source line is only a passing nod to grounding.","The claim's main action is choosing a check by running several candidates and keeping the best, with a reusable file that lists its blind spots. That is check-selection craft, not H's grounding idea (the gap gets filled confidently and wrong), A's safety-needs-a-check idea, or C's fixed judge for measuring change. The source-comparison line touches grounding but is secondary.","Opening the cited sources and comparing them to the claim is grounding (staying connected to the truth). The 'run several checks, keep the winner' part matches the old M5 page, not an eval with a fixed judge."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"The AI makes things up when it has no source, and asking it 'are you sure' does not help. You have to check the report against the real source file, and Claude can build a checker for that which you reuse.","claims":["When the AI hands you a report and there is no document it was reading from, assume parts of it are invented, and don't trust it just because it says it is confident. Compare each statement in the report with the original file yourself, and ask the AI to write you a small script that does that comparison automatically, so you can run it on every future report.","When an AI gives you a report, don't ask it to confirm itself; compare its claims against the original source documents. Have the AI write a reusable checking tool for that comparison and run it each time.","When the AI writes a report that depends on documents, never trust its own word that the report is correct, and never settle it by asking it to double-check itself. Compare the report's statements against the original files yourself, and have the AI write a small script that does this comparison automatically, then run that script every time you get a new report.","When the AI gives you a report and there is no real document behind it, it will invent details that sound right, and asking it to confirm itself changes nothing. Compare its report line by line against the original file, and have the AI write you a small reusable program that does this comparison, then run that program each time."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["No source to read from means the AI fills gaps with confident, plausible, wrong content; the claim says stay connected to the original file and compare against it, which is grounding.","No source document means the AI fills the gap with confident, plausible invention; the fix is checking claims against ground truth (grounding), not trusting stated confidence.","Comparing the report's claims against original source documents is grounding: staying connected to the truth rather than trusting the AI's self-confirmation. The reusable checker is just how that grounding gets done.","The claim says not to trust the AI's self-confirmation but to run an independent, repeatable check each time. That is D's 'you can't tell from the output; run the check'. G is about connecting the agent to the ground truth, which the claim doesn't say.","Checking the report against the source files yourself, with a script, keeps it tied to the ground truth instead of trusting the AI's confident claim; this is grounding.","The claim is about checking a document-dependent report against the original files instead of trusting the AI's own word, which is grounding; idea A is about safety, which the claim doesn't mention.","Invented plausible details when no real document grounds the report; self-confirmation doesn't help; fix is to check against the source file (grounding).","Without a real document it invents plausible details, and the fix is to check against the original source, which is grounding. The reusable comparison program is how you run the check."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust the AI's own word. Check it against the source, and try a few different checks, because each one finds different mistakes, like different inspectors at a depot. Too many big words for me, but that part I could use for a delivery report.","claims":["Don't accept an AI's statement just because it says so. Compare its output against the original data, and run several different kinds of checks, since each kind catches errors the others miss.","When an AI gives you a result, don't accept it on its say-so: compare it against the original data, and run several different kinds of checks, since each kind catches a different sort of error. For a delivery report, verify the numbers against the source records in more than one way before sending.","When an AI gives you a result, don't accept it on its say-so. Compare it to the original data, and run several different kinds of checks, since each kind catches different errors. Do this before sending out something like a delivery report.","When an AI gives you a result, don't accept it on its say-so: compare it with the original data, and run several different kinds of checks, since each kind catches errors the others miss."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","output-quality"],"why":["Not trusting the AI's say-so and checking its output against the original data is grounding (staying connected to the truth).","Verifying AI output against the original data (the ground truth) is grounding; the 'several kinds of checks' detail is incidental.","Comparing the AI's result to the original data is grounding: staying connected to the truth source instead of trusting the agent's say-so.","Compare AI output to the original data (ground truth) rather than trusting it on its say-so is grounding; the 'several kinds of checks' is a supporting detail, not the safety-check idea of H.","The claim says to compare an AI's output against the original source data instead of trusting it. That is grounding, staying connected to the truth, and the claim is about accuracy, not safety.","The core act is checking the AI's output against the original source records (the ground truth) instead of trusting it, which is the grounding idea; the claim is about accuracy, not safety.","The claim says not to trust the output on its say-so and to run checks on it. That is D's core. It does not say the agent invents answers when it can't reach the source, which is what H adds.","The claim's core is checking an AI result against the original data, the ground truth, instead of trusting it. 'Several kinds of checks' is a method for doing that. B is about whether an agent is safe, which is a different topic."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"AI makes things up when it has no real source, and asking it 'are you sure' does not help. You check the output against the real file, and a checker only catches invented things, not a wrong source.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's own confirmation of its work, because it will invent details when it lacks a real source, and asking it to double-check gets you nothing. Compare what it produced against the actual source document yourself. That comparison will catch made-up content, but it won't tell you if you gave it the wrong source in the first place, so choose the source carefully.","When the AI has no real source to draw on, it will invent content. Asking it to double-check itself won't fix that. Compare what it produced against the actual file or document. That comparison only exposes invented details; if the source you gave it was wrong, the comparison won't catch that, so confirm the source separately.","When the AI has no real document to draw from, it will invent content. Asking it to double-check itself is useless. Compare what it produced against the actual source file yourself. Also remember that this comparison only exposes fabricated details; if the source you compared against is itself the wrong one, the check will pass anyway.","AI will invent details when it has nothing real to draw on, and re-asking it whether it is certain won't fix that. Next week, compare what it produced against the actual source file yourself. Know that this comparison only exposes made-up content; if the source you compared against is itself wrong, the comparison will still pass, so make sure the source is the right one."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["Invented content when there is no source, and checking output against the real source (while confirming the source itself is true) is the grounding idea.","Invented content when no real source exists, checking output against the actual file, and verifying the source itself is grounding: staying connected to the truth.","Fabrication when no real source exists, plus checking against ground truth (and the caveat that a wrong source passes) is the grounding idea: stay connected to truth, gaps get filled plausibly wrong.","Invented content when there's no real source, and the fix is checking against ground truth (with the caveat that the source must be the right one) — that's grounding.","Fabricates when lacking a real source, so compare against the actual source (ground truth), with the caveat that the source must be right: grounding.","Invented details when it lacks a real source, and verifying against the actual source (ground truth), is the grounding idea.","AI fills gaps with invented details when it can't reach real data; the fix is checking against the actual source (grounding), and the caveat is to make sure the source is the right one.","Invents details when it can't reach real data, verifying against the source (not self-certainty) and ensuring the source is right is grounding."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-ops-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Mistakes add up when a report goes through many steps. 85% right per step is only about 20% right at the end, so you have to check against the real file or number, not ask the AI 'are you sure?'","claims":["When a result is built through a chain of several steps, assume the errors pile up: even a high per-step accuracy (around 85%) leaves only about a fifth of the final output correct. So before trusting the end result, compare it with the original source (the actual file or figure), and don't treat the AI saying it is confident as verification.","When a task passes through many stages, small error rates at each stage multiply into a large overall error rate, so don't rely on the AI to confirm its own work by asking if it is sure. Instead, at each stage compare the output to the actual source file or figure.","When a result comes out of a chain of many steps, expect it to be much less reliable than any single step, because small error rates multiply. Don't ask the AI to confirm itself; open the original file or figure and compare the output against it.","When a task runs through many stages, small error rates at each stage multiply, so the final result can be mostly wrong even if every stage looks mostly fine. Don't ask the AI to confirm its own work; compare its output to the original source, such as the actual file or figure."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","security","output-quality","security"],"why":["The claim is about checking each stage's output against the actual source file or figure rather than the AI's self-confirmation, which is grounding: staying connected to the truth.","Compounding stage errors plus checking output against the actual source (the ground) instead of the AI's self-confirmation is grounding: staying connected to truth.","Errors compounding across a chain and checking output against the original source instead of self-confirmation is about staying connected to ground truth (grounding), not just running a safety check.","Compounding error across many steps plus verifying against the original source (not self-confirmation) is about staying connected to ground truth; A is about checking safety of an agent, not chain reliability or source comparison.","Errors compounding in a chain, checking against the original source, and distrusting the AI's stated confidence is grounding: staying connected to truth because the agent fills gaps confidently and wrongly.","The claim is about not trusting output or the AI's stated confidence and running a check against the original source, which is the verification practice in C, not just staying connected to ground truth as in E.","Comparing output to the original source (the file or figure) is grounding: stay connected to the truth rather than letting the AI self-confirm, which is what the claim's second sentence says.","Compounding stage errors plus 'don't ask the AI to confirm itself; compare against the original' means run an external check, since output alone can't show safety or correctness."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"The tool makes things up when it has no source, so a check only counts if it goes back to the source files, and you should test which checker works on your own material instead of trusting anyone's word. It was a lot of vocabulary, though.","claims":["When the AI has nothing real to draw on, it invents answers, so any verification step must compare the output against the original source material. Before relying on a verifier, try the candidates on your own data and see which one catches the invented parts; don't take anyone's recommendation on faith.","When the AI has no document to draw on, it will invent an answer. So when you want to verify its output, use a check that goes back to your original files and compares the output against them. Before you rely on any checking method, try several on your own data and keep the one that catches the errors there.","When the AI has no source to draw on, it invents answers, so any check you rely on must compare the output against the original source files. Before trusting a checker, run the candidates on your own material and see which one catches real errors.","When the AI has no document to draw on, it invents answers. So when you check its work, make the check compare against your original source files, not just re-read the AI's own output. Before relying on any checking method, run a few of them on your own real material and see which one actually catches the invented parts."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim says the AI invents answers when it has no source document, and that you should check its output against your original files. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, with the gap made visible.","The claim centers on the AI inventing answers when it lacks source documents and on checking against the original files, which is grounding (H). The 'try several checks, keep the one that works' part is incidental.","The claim says the AI invents answers when it has no source and that checks must compare output against the original source files. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, because the agent fills gaps with plausible wrong answers.","No source → invents answers, and checks must compare against the original source: grounding, staying connected to truth so the gap becomes visible.","Invented answers when the agent can't reach real source material, with verification against the original source, is grounding (G); the 'try candidates on your own data' part is incidental.","Invented answers when the agent has no real source to draw on, plus checking output against the original source, is grounding. The 'try candidates on your own data' part is an extra detail and doesn't change the core idea.","No source to draw on means it invents answers; check against original source files (the ground) to make the gap visible. That is grounding.","Without a source to draw on the AI fills gaps with plausible inventions; checking against original source files is grounding, which makes the gap visible."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"A confident AI answer with no source looks just like a grounded one, so 'are you sure' proves nothing. You need a checker that touches the sources, and you can test checkers on your own material and read the scoreboard instead of taking anyone's word for it.","claims":["When an AI gives a confident answer without showing a source, don't treat asking it to confirm as a check. Use a separate checker that opens the cited sources and compares them to the answer. Before trusting any checker, run it on your own material and compare the scores it gets, rather than relying on someone's claim that it works.","When an AI gives you a confident answer, don't trust it because it sounds sure or because it says yes when you ask if it's certain. Use a separate check that actually opens the original sources and compares them to the answer. Before relying on any such check, try several candidates on your own data and compare their results in a visible score table, then pick the one that scores best.","When an AI gives a confident answer without a source, don't trust it just because you ask it to confirm; instead use a separate check that actually opens the cited sources and compares them to the answer. Before relying on any such check, try it on your own material and compare the results across checkers, so you pick the one that performs best instead of the one that's recommended.","When an AI gives you a confident answer without showing its sources, don't trust it just because it sounds sure, and don't settle for asking it to confirm. Use a separate check that actually opens and compares the cited sources, and try out candidate checks on your own material, comparing their results side by side, before relying on any of them."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim is about a confident answer with no source, and the fix is to check it against the cited sources. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth, because the gap gets filled confidently and wrong. Validating the checker on your own material is only a supporting step.","The claim is about confident answers with no source: check them by opening the cited sources, not by asking the AI to confirm. That is grounding, with the gap made visible.","The core is confident answers with no source: don't self-confirm, check against the actual sources to stay grounded. Picking the best checker is secondary.","The core act is checking a confident, unsourced answer against the actual cited sources instead of asking the model to confirm itself, which is grounding. Testing checkers on your own material is a secondary step.","Confident-sounding answers aren't proof; the claim says to check them against the original sources, which is grounding. The 'try several checks, keep the winner' part is an extra, not the main idea.","The core is to distrust confident answers and check them against the original sources (staying connected to the ground). The pick-the-best-check score table is a secondary step, not an eval of whether a change improved the work.","The claim says to distrust a confident answer with no sources and to check it by opening and comparing the cited sources. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth, where the agent would otherwise fill the gap confidently.","The claim is about not trusting a confident answer and checking it against the actual sources (grounding). The 'try candidate checks side by side' part is a method, not the eval idea of a fixed judge measuring change."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Don't trust the AI to check itself. Any check has to go back to the source, and you should test the checkers on your own material to see what each one misses, because errors pile up over a long chain and no single checker catches everything.","claims":["Never accept an AI's own review of its work as proof. Make every check compare the output against the original source material. Before relying on any checker, run it on your own real examples and note what it misses, since each one misses different things. Run several of them in sequence, because mistakes accumulate across long chains of steps.","Never let an AI vouch for its own work. Verify every output against the original source material. Before relying on any verification step, run it on your own data with known problems to see which kinds of mistakes it misses. Use several different checks, because mistakes accumulate across a long sequence of steps and no single check catches them all.","When an AI produces a result and you need to know if it's right, don't ask the same AI to review it. Compare the result against the original source material (the real documents or data). Before relying on any reviewer, whether a person, a script, or another AI, run it on a sample of your own work where you already know the errors, and note which kinds of mistakes it misses. Use several different reviewers, because mistakes accumulate across many steps and no one reviewer catches all of them.","Don't let the AI be the one that grades its own work. Every verification step must compare the output against the original source material. Before relying on any verification step, run it on your own data where you know what the mistakes are, and see which ones it misses. Expect mistakes to accumulate across a long series of steps, so use several different verification steps rather than trusting one."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","security","none","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about running a check rather than trusting the agent's own output: verify against source, test the verifier on known problems, and use several checks. That matches F, which says you can't judge safety from output alone and have to run the check.","The claim is about not trusting the agent's self-report and running checks, testing the checker on known problems, and layering several checks. That is the 'you can't tell if it's safe from its output, run the check' idea. The 'verify against source material' part could point toward grounding (G), but the stress on testing and running checks is D.","The core is to check output against the original source, not the AI's own review. That is staying connected to the truth, and the claim says nothing about safety or about measuring improvement with a fixed judge.","The claim is about verifying output against source and testing checkers: running the check rather than trusting the agent's own output. F (grounding) is close but the claim centres on checking, not the gap-filling/staying-connected idea.","The claim is about verification practice (independent judges, source comparison, testing checks against known errors, layered checks); E is about grounding to truth, A about fixed-judge evals, and G about running safety checks, none matching in substance.","The claim is about checking safety/correctness by running independent checks and testing them on known errors, not trusting the agent's own output, which matches 'run the check' rather than grounding or evals.","Core is don't trust output or same-AI review; run checks and test reviewers on known errors with varied reviewers, matching 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","Core is that you can't judge safety/correctness from the output itself: validate the reviewer by running it on known errors and use independent checks, i.e. run the check."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"s1-veteran-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"s1","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"8f87cfc274a54e95","answer":"Asking the tool 'are you sure?' proves nothing; a check has to touch the source, and you can test which checker works on your own material with planted errors and a score. I'd still not call it the future, but the scoreboard was worth the hour.","claims":["Don't rely on asking the AI to double-check its own work; use a check that goes back to the original source material. Before trusting any checker, try it on your own data with deliberately inserted mistakes and count how many it catches, so you know which one works.","Don't ask the AI to re-check its own work by saying 'are you sure?'; make the check look at the original source material. To pick which checker to trust, seed some known mistakes into your own documents and see which checker finds the most, scoring each one.","Don't ask the AI to double-check its own work by saying 'are you sure?'. Make the check look at the original source material. To pick the best checker, plant known mistakes in your own documents, run each candidate checker, and score how many it catches.","Don't trust an AI's own 'are you sure?' reply as verification. Use a check that goes back to the original source material. To find out which checking method works on your data, plant known mistakes in it and score how many each method catches."],"picks":["output-quality","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The core is to check against the original source material, not the model's self-confidence. That is staying connected to the ground truth. Seeding known errors is only a way to pick the checker, not the idea of an eval.","The claim is about verification: a self-check like 'are you sure?' doesn't work, so use a real check against the source and test which checker catches planted errors.","The core is checking against the original source material, not the model's own say-so, which is grounding. The seeded-error test only helps pick a checker that actually reaches the source.","The claim says to check against the original source material instead of asking the AI to re-check itself. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth. The planted-mistakes test only decides which grounded checker to trust.","The claim says to check against the original source material instead of the AI's self-assessment, which is grounding: staying connected to the truth. Planting known errors is how you test that the check works.","Verifying against the original source material (not the model's self-check) is grounding; the planted-errors test is just how to pick the method.","The core action is to check against the original source rather than ask the model 'are you sure?', which is grounding. Planting errors to compare checkers is the mechanism, not the idea.","The core move is to make the check look at the original source instead of asking the model to re-judge itself. That is grounding. Planting errors to compare checkers is only the method for choosing one."],"score":0.875,"hit":false,"half":true}
